al": 5.25, "baseline_margin": 5.31, "analyst_adjustment": -0.31, "key_factors": ["Consistent polling lead for El-Sayed, averaging about 5 points across 54 surveys", "Prediction markets price El-Sayed near 79%, aligning with a Likely D read", "Open seat after Peters' retirement removes incumbency advantage", "Stevens' surprise endorsement of Rogers adds late-cycle uncertainty"], "rationale": "Polling averages cluster around a 5-point El-Sayed lead and both Kalshi and Polymarket price him near 79%, supporting a Likely D rating despite expert ratings split between Tossup and Lean/Likely D. The open seat and heavy outside spending against El-Sayed keep uncertainty elevated, but no recent poll shows Rogers ahead.", "watch": "Whether the Haley Stevens endorsement of Rogers shifts any late votes toward Republicans, or a final round of polls narrows the race into Tossup territory.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Gary Peters (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Michigan's open Senate seat pits Democrat Abdul El-Sayed against Republican Mike Rogers after Gary Peters' retirement. Polls conducted in late October consistently show El-Sayed ahead, with margins ranging from two to eleven points and an average near five. Prediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket both price El-Sayed at roughly 79% to win, and most quantitative averages land in the same range.\n\nThe race has drawn heavy outside spending against El-Sayed, and a surprise endorsement of Rogers by his defeated primary rival Haley Stevens injected late drama. Expert ratings are split between Tossup and Lean or Likely D, reflecting a competitive but Democratic-tilted contest in a politically even state. The forecast expects El-Sayed to win by about five points, with meaningful residual uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7769, "margin": 3.59}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7646, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7534, "margin": 3.2}, {"date": "2026-09-25